A long time ago, I posted a question about fixing Omega's restore-game
bug for compilation with SunOS 4.1.1. A kind colleague found the
troublesome code, and provided a simple patch. I'm not familiar with
the 'diff' utility, so I merely used it to create so simple
instructions on how to execute the patch:

The problem is in the source file 'osave.c'. Open the file and
cruise down to line 607. You will find the following lines: 

     Pawnitems[i] = restore_item(fd);
   Condoitems = restore_itemlist(fd);
   for (i=0;iTOTALITEMS;i++) 
 #ifndef MSDOS
     fread((char *)&(Objects[i].known),sizeof(int),1,fd);
 #else
     fread((char *)&(Objects[i].known),sizeof(Objects[i].known),1,fd);
 #endif
   calc_melee();
 }

See where it says 'sizeof(int)' above? That's the problem. Change it
to read:

       Pawnitems[i] = restore_item(fd);
     Condoitems = restore_itemlist(fd);
     for (i=0;i<TOTALITEMS;i++) 
       fread((char *)&(Objects[i].known),sizeof(Objects[i].known),1,fd);
     calc_melee();
   }

(Since we're not compiling for MSDOS, I just took the #def out
entirely.)

Ta da! I compiled with gcc 2.0 (-traditional) and it works just fine!

